    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Buddhist dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> for about four centuries, from around 750 to around 1161.</li><li>Its kings founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara">Somapura</a> and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, the great monastic universities of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>Pala scholars and art shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> for Tibet.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a> granted five villages to support a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaputradeva">Balaputradeva</a>, the Shailendra king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvarnadvipa">Suvarnadvipa</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>), at that king’s request.</p></aside>
    </section>

