    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shashanka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shashanka.jpg" alt="Shashanka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gauda_ruler_Sasanka_Deva._Circa_AD_600-635._Samatata_type._Assam_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pala Empire arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in the middle of the eighth century after a long period of disorder that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a>, king of Gauda, in the early seventh century and the collapse of later regional powers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalimpur-copper-plate">Khalimpur copper plate</a> of Dharmapala describes this period as <em>matsyanyaya</em>, ‘the law of the fish’, in which the big devoured the small, and records that the people, the <em>prakriti</em>, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gopala-pala-dynasty">Gopala</a> as king to end it. Whether this was a genuine election by local chiefs or a later legitimising story is uncertain, but Gopala, a Buddhist of modest origins, established a kingdom in Bengal around 750. His son Dharmapala (r. c. 770–810) turned it into an empire. He extended Pala rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, pushed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley and, after setbacks against the Gurjara-Pratihara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> and the Rashtrakuta Dhruva, installed his own candidate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a>, on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>. According to the Khalimpur plate, the kings of the Bhojas, Matsyas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kurus</a>, Yadus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a>, Avanti, Gandhara and Kira attended the ceremony and approved it, a claim that at least shows the scale of Dharmapala’s ambitions. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a> (r. c. 810–850) consolidated these gains and made the Pala kingdom the strongest power in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara.jpg" alt="Somapura Mahavihara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara">Somapura Mahavihara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paharpur_Buddhist_Bihar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abdulmominbd, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pala lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> were among the most fertile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and other crops in the deltas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants record the gift of villages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and Buddhist monasteries, with careful descriptions of fields, ponds and boundaries. The economy was largely agrarian, and Pala coinage is scarce, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells served as small currency in Bengal. An Arab merchant writing in 851 described a king called Rahma, usually identified with the Palas, as possessing vast numbers of elephants and a land producing cotton cloth so fine that a garment could pass through a ring. The dynasty was Buddhist, its kings styling themselves <em>paramasaugata</em>, devoted followers of the Buddha, and it supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana Buddhism</a> on a scale unmatched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>. Dharmapala founded the monastic university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> and the great monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara">Somapura</a> at Paharpur, in present-day Bangladesh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a> made grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, including five villages granted at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaputradeva">Balaputradeva</a>, the Shailendra king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suvarnadvipa">Suvarnadvipa</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>), for a monastery he had built there. The Palas also endowed Brahmins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, and their ministers came from Brahmin families. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charyapada">Charyapada</a></em>, Buddhist mystical songs usually dated between the eighth and twelfth centuries, are among the earliest works in an ancestor of Bengali.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/govinda-iii.jpg" alt="Govinda III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscription_of_Rashtrakuta_Emperor_Govinda_III_at_Veerabhadra_temple,_Mavali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pala armies relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the eastern forests were famous, together with infantry, cavalry obtained largely from the north and west, and river fleets suited to the waterways of the delta; inscriptions mention boats as well as elephant and horse forces in the royal camps. For much of the late eighth and ninth centuries the Palas fought the tripartite struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> with the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty and the Rashtrakuta dynasty. Dharmapala was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> and then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, yet managed to place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a> on the throne of Kannauj after both rivals withdrew; around 800 the Rashtrakuta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> marched north and received the submission of both Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a> defeated Dharmapala near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munger">Monghyr</a> and took Kannauj. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a>’s inscriptions claim victories over the Utkalas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Hunas</a>, the Dravidas and the rulers of Pragjyotisha in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, claims that are exaggerated but suggest real campaigns to the south and east. From the late ninth century the Pratiharas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendrapala-i">Mahendrapala I</a> pushed the Palas back and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> for a time. In the eleventh century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra</a> I sent an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> around 1021–1023 that claims to have defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-i-pala">Mahipala I</a>, and later the Kalachuris and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Kalyani raided Pala territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pataliputra.jpg" alt="Pataliputra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pataliputra6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyule, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pala kings ruled as hereditary monarchs with the imperial titles <em>Parameshvara</em>, <em>Paramabhattaraka</em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a></em>, and they moved between victory camps, <em>jayaskandhavara</em>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munger">Mudgagiri</a> (Monghyr) and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramavati">Ramavati</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a>, rather than holding a single fixed capital. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grants list a long hierarchy of officials, from ministers and generals to revenue officers, police and keepers of forts, ferries and elephants, and they divided the kingdom into provinces called <em>bhukti</em>, and further into <em>mandala</em> and <em>vishaya</em>. Despite their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, the Palas relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> ministers: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badal-pillar-inscription">Badal pillar inscription</a> records a family that served successive kings as chief ministers, including Darbhapani and Kedaramishra. Royal power beyond the core lands depended on feudatories, the <em>samantas</em>, who held territory in return for tribute and military service, and the reliance on them grew over time. This became decisive under Ramapala, who recovered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varendra-rebellion">Kaivarta</a> rebels only with the support of a coalition of samantas whose names are recorded in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramacharita">Ramacharita</a></em> of Sandhyakara Nandi. The longevity of the dynasty, which ruled for about four centuries through roughly eighteen kings, owed much to this flexible structure and to the agricultural wealth of its heartland, but it also meant that local lords could break away when the centre was weak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="Nalanda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Odantapuribs, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Palas were the last great royal patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and their influence on the Buddhist world was immense. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara">Somapura</a> and Odantapuri attracted monks and students from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and Tibetan tradition holds that the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a>, Tibet’s first, was modelled on Odantapuri. Scholars trained in Pala monasteries carried Indian Buddhist texts and practices abroad, and the most celebrated of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana</a>, left Vikramashila around 1040 and spent his last years in Tibet, where his teaching helped shape the later spread of Buddhism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadam-school">Kadam school</a>. Pala art was equally influential. The bronze and black-stone sculpture of eastern India, which the Tibetan historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranatha">Taranatha</a> associated with the artists Dhiman and his son Bitpalo, and the illustrated palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Buddhist texts such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita">Prajnaparamita</a></em>, provided models for the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Tibet, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. The Shailendra connection seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a>’s Nalanda grant illustrates these links. Within India the Palas made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> a major political power for the first time since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashanka">Shashanka</a>, and the scripts used in their inscriptions developed into the eastern scripts from which Bengali and Assamese writing descend. The ruins of Somapura <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> at Paharpur became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1985, and the remains of Nalanda followed in 2016.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/varendra-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varendra-rebellion.jpg" alt="Varendra rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/varendra-rebellion">Varendra rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varendri.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stormmaashrooms, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devapala-pala-emperor">Devapala</a>, Pala power declined under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanapala">Narayanapala</a> and his successors. The Pratiharas occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, the Rashtrakutas raided from the south and in the tenth century a Kamboja dynasty seized northern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, leaving the Palas with only part of their territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-i-pala">Mahipala I</a>, who reigned from about 988 to about 1038, revived the empire by recovering Bengal, and his long reign saw building and renewed patronage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> raid of 1021–1023 and pressure from the Kalachuris showed its vulnerability. In the second half of the eleventh century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varendra-rebellion">Kaivartas</a>, a community of boatmen and cultivators in northern Bengal, rose under their leader Divya, killing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-ii">Mahipala II</a> and holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varendra">Varendra</a> for a generation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramapala">Ramapala</a> (r. c. 1077–1130) suppressed them with the help of his samantas and restored the kingdom, extending its influence to Kamarupa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, but the dependence on feudatories outlasted him. Among them were the Senas, of southern Indian origin, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayasena">Vijayasena</a> made himself master of Bengal in the mid-twelfth century. The last Pala kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madanapala">Madanapala</a>, held only parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, and Madanapala’s death around 1161 marks the end of the dynasty. Within a few decades the Turkish campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a> destroyed the great monasteries of Bihar, including Nalanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Pala Empire made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> a great power for four centuries and was the last major royal patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, sustaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara">Somapura</a>, whose scholars and art carried Indian Buddhism to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</em></p>

